<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dušan's Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1349">1349</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Zakonik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dušan's Code was the law code of the Serbian Empire, adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> at an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> in 1349 and enlarged in 1354. It set out the rights of the Church, the nobility and the peasantry and drew on Byzantine law. It is one of the most important legal monuments of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dusans-code.jpg" alt="Dušan's Code" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vukašin of Serbia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Du%C5%A1an%27s_Code,_Prizren_manuscript,_15th_c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, 16 April 1346, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> held a great assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> at which the Serbian Archbishopric was raised to a patriarchate, and the first Serbian Patriarch, Joanikije II, crowned Dušan Emperor and autocrat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The Code was promulgated at a state council in Skopje on 21 May 1349, in the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> synod, the patriarch, the clergy and the lords of the empire. A second part, about half the size of the first, was added later at a council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a>.</p><p>The Code had 201 articles in all. Four of them invoke the Law of the Sainted King, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Uroš II Milutin</a>, which suggests that a lost code of Milutin existed. Most early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> pair Dušan's Code with an abridged Syntagma of Matthew Blastares and a short so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-justinian">Code of Justinian</a>, drawn mostly from the Farmer's Law, and scholars such as A. Solovjev and Soulis hold that the council issued all three together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Since civil and criminal law were covered by the other two parts, Dušan's articles dealt mainly with public law and procedure: courts and their jurisdictions, ordeals and juries, taxes, grazing rights, landholding, inheritance, and the position of slaves and serfs. Serfs owed labour dues but could bring complaints against their lord before the Emperor's court. Byzantine influence brought executions and mutilations in place of traditional fines. Punishment depended on social class, and commoners were forbidden to hold councils on pain of having their ears cut off.</p><p>The Church held a privileged position but was bound to feed the poor, and simony was banned. Catholics were called half believers, Catholic proselytism and mixed marriages were forbidden, and heretics such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a> were strongly penalised. Villages and frontier lords were made liable for brigands and raiders in their lands, which the historian Fine reads as a sign of the state's weak hold on rural and border areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Code remained in force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> and in the Serbian Despotate until its annexation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1459. Serbian communities under Ottoman rule, and areas under the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> such as Grbalj and Paštrovići, continued to refer to it. The original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is lost; 24 manuscripts are known today, the oldest the incomplete Struga manuscript of 1373, and the most complete the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a>, Bistrica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> texts of the 15th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dušan's Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dušan's Code</b> was enacted at Skopje on 21 May 1349 and at Serres in 1354 by assemblies of bishops, nobles and provincial governors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
